## Proof

We evaluate
$$I = \int_0^\infty \frac{\ln x}{\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\,dx.$$

### Convergence (brief)

Near $x=0$: the integrand is $\sim x^{-1/2}\ln x$, which is integrable on $(0,1)$ since $\int_0^1 x^{-1/2}|\ln x|\,dx < \infty$. Near $x=\infty$: the integrand is $\sim \ln x / x^{3/2}$, which is integrable on $(1,\infty)$. So $I$ converges.

### Substitution $x = t^2$

Let $x = t^2$, so $dx = 2t\,dt$, $\sqrt{x}=t$, $\ln x = 2\ln t$, and $x+1 = t^2+1$. Then
$$I = \int_0^\infty \frac{2\ln t}{t(t^2+1)}\cdot 2t\,dt = 4\int_0^\infty \frac{\ln t}{t^2+1}\,dt = 4J,$$
where $J := \int_0^\infty \frac{\ln t}{t^2+1}\,dt$.

### Evaluating $J$ via the involution $t \mapsto 1/t$

Substitute $t = 1/s$, $dt = -s^{-2}\,ds$:
$$J = \int_\infty^0 \frac{\ln(1/s)}{1/s^2+1}\left(-\frac{1}{s^2}\right)ds = \int_0^\infty \frac{-\ln s}{(1+s^2)/s^2}\cdot\frac{1}{s^2}\,ds = -\int_0^\infty \frac{\ln s}{1+s^2}\,ds = -J.$$

Hence $J = -J$, so $2J = 0$, giving $J = 0$.

### Conclusion

$$I = 4J = 4 \cdot 0 = \boxed{0}.$$

